OLD before replacing it. A long mobile background suspension forces replacement because the OS can kill a socket without reporting closure. Treating every foreground event as a reconnect delays healthy attempts; treating every resume as harmless leaves suspended sockets stuck. The
NEW reports are often wrong, for example for a loopback server. The OS can kill a suspended socket without a close event, so a probe still unanswered after 500 ms asks the host for its public descriptor over a fresh request, again each second until it answers. If the host answers while the socket delivers no frame, the socket is dead and is replaced, even while the network reports offline: at once on loopback and LAN, and 2.5 seconds after the check starts elsewhere, where one lost packet can stall a live socket. Without that evidence the probe deadline decides: 3 seconds on loopback and LAN routes and after a long mobile suspension, when the data on screen is stale; 15 seconds elsewhere, because a tailnet over DERP or a VPN can be slower than that on a live socket. A long suspension's probe that times out also replaces the socket regardless of the network report. The web and desktop foreground probe skips the fresh request, because a busy host can answer it before a queued probe. Foregrounding
