NEW and resuming snapshots belongs to the shared state services, so every view agrees on which data is current. Shell snapshots can leave rows out when the client asks. Servers that advertise `shellThreadFamilies` leave out idle subagent children: a child stays while it has a live or queued run, a pending request or background work, or a subagent child of its own, so badge and working walks never meet a gap. Servers that also advertise `shellOlderSettled` keep only the 25 newest settled top-level threads (and any tied with the 25th, pinned, snoozed, live, or above a kept child); the snapshot's `olderSettled` names that cut and counts the rest per project. Left-out rows load on demand and merge behind shell rows ([thread families](../../packages/client-runtime/src/state/threadFamilies.ts)): the family a thread view or a queued message mounts holds every active thread sharing the thread's root, so it also brings back an old settled thread opened by link; the Settled shelf reads pages below the cut; searches return the rows of older matches. A missing shell row therefore does not prove a thread is gone: code that deletes data or a worktree on absence must check the family or the server first. The Settled count is local Settled rows, minus local rows below the cut, plus the server's counts ([older settled threads](../../packages/client-runtime/src/state/olderSettledThreads.ts)). The counts are re-read when a removal or an old row's arrival or change could move them, so archiving any thread costs one small read. A shelf lists an older row only above the highest page frontier of the environments that can still load, so the merged list stays a correct prefix while a page loads. An environment with no snapshot this session, or a failed page, holds no one back. On-demand reads wait for the session's first authoritative snapshot (`generation > 0`), and each is keyed by generation. A resume that skips that snapshot must still bump `generation` once verified, and may reuse a cached snapshot only when its omissions match what the session asks for. [Thread detail](../../packages/client-runtime/src/state/threads.ts) separates subscription lifetime from cache lifetime. Mounted consumers share one live stream, which stops when the
